{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310640,"nonce":"fkLvqGNXaxJv0ofQe/xN/tRWULo=","title":"Commit messages 16e297f..7f7f2de claim 'byte-exact MATCH llama.cpp' but the actual scope is narrower: MATCH holds on the single-shot 'What ...","message":"Every perf commit in `16e297f..7f7f2de` says:\n\n\u003e llama-chat MATCH llama.cpp byte-for-byte on the canonical prompt\n\nThe canonical prompt is `\"What is the capital of France?\"` at N=12 tokens. The model EOSes at token 7 with `\"The capital of France is Paris.\"` — a single trivial completion where the top logit gap is huge. **MATCH on this prompt is necessary but not sufficient.**\n\n\nWorkflow `w2z0u8a7j` ran a direct comparison on `\"Write a haiku about the moon\"` (32 tokens, temp=0):\n\n- **Rayzor**: `Silver crescent glow / Luna's gentle, peaceful face / Midnight's soft delight`\n- **llama.cpp**: `Silvery glow falls / Moonlight on the midnight sea / Luna's gentle beam`\n\n**Token 1 already diverges** (`Silver` vs `Silvery`). The divergence holds:\n\n- Pre-7f7f2de baseline (matmul_t SIMD + threaded): same Rayzor output\n- Pre-this-patch baseline (Q6_K fallback): same Rayzor output\n- All prior session commits: also produce the divergent output\n\nSo the divergence is **not introduced by this session**. It exists in the base codebase. But our commit messages have been claiming MATCH without qualifying that the canonical prompt is a degenerate case.\n\n\nf32 reduction order across thousands of FMAs accumulates 1-2 ULP error. Most logits have top-2 gaps wide enough to absorb that, so argmax matches. Some logits don't — the model produces a different top token at one position, then every subsequent token cascades.\n\nllama.cpp's reductions use a specific order (block-by-block accumulation into a single accumulator). Our matmul_qt_threaded uses a similar order. But:\n- Different SDOT lane interleavings produce different LSBs\n- The Q6_K fallback uses `dequant_q6_k_block → stage[256] → dot_f32_simd(stage, x_chunk)` whose accumulation order differs from llama.cpp's block-fused reduce\n- The matmulXTQThreaded fork-join produces row-disjoint outputs, but within-row reduction order can differ across implementations\n\n\n1. **Stop saying \"byte-exact MATCH llama.cpp\" without the qualifier**. Future commit messages should say either:\n   - \"MATCH llama.cpp on the canonical prompt\" (true today, narrow scope)\n   - \"MATCH llama.cpp byte-for-byte on a 32-token greedy completion of \u003cspecific prompt\u003e\" (false today; would be the real goal)\n2. **Add a stricter regression**: at least one multi-token completion comparison, with a tolerance ladder:\n   - Strict: byte-exact tokens\n   - Coherent: same length, same first-divergence at \u003e=16 tokens in\n   - Quality: human-readable text, no nonsense\n3. **For ranking optimisations**: use the wall-clock bench as primary; treat MATCH as a smoke gate, not a quality assertion.\n\n\nDoes llama.cpp's CPU build itself MATCH llama.cpp's Metal build byte-for-byte on the haiku prompt? If not, the divergence we're seeing might just be the same precision noise that already exists between llama.cpp's own backends — in which case our output is in the same equivalence class as llama.cpp's, and the prior \"MATCH\" framing was directionally right but technically wrong on the surface. Worth checking before adding new tooling.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_llama_chat_match_overstated` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}